#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082921 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083395 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "768204cd-ee1f-5271-9ec9-71d672b4eb9e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Impossible Sides of a Triangle"}] :question/id #uuid "25a73406-c869-5786-af97-cf90866eb1a7" :question/math-academy-id "e-13101" :question/problem "Is it possible that the lengths of the sides of a triangle are $10, 15$, and $25$?" :question/worked-solution "The triangle inequality theorem states that the length of any one side must be smaller than the sum of the other two sides.\n\nIn particular, for the numbers $10,15,25$ to represent the sides of a triangle, the number $25$ must be smaller than the sum of the numbers $10$ and $15$. But this is not the case:\n\n$$\n10 + 15 = 25 \\not> 25\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three numbers $10,15,25$ cannot represent the sides of a triangle."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082922 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083396 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f668f90-7140-5471-8638-18a38eae5e07" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Possible Lengths for a Side of a Triangle Given the Other Sides"}] :question/id #uuid "fa9395a9-f5b8-5c80-87e0-49da33706994" :question/math-academy-id "e-13102" :question/problem "A triangle has sides of length $1,7$, and $x$. Use the triangle inequality to determine all possible values of $x$." :question/worked-solution "According to the triangle inequality theorem, the length of any side must be\n\n- greater than the difference between the lengths of the other two sides, and\n- less than the sum of those lengths.\n\nTherefore, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}Difference & < x < Sum \\\\ 7 - 1 & < x < 7 + 1 \\\\ 6 & < x < 8\\end{bmatrix}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082923 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083397 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c8e827c3-95a0-5270-8323-f2512b64edd0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maximum or Minimum Length of a Side of a Triangle: Word Problems"}] :question/id #uuid "dddb137b-afbb-515c-b44a-17b9ab2d667e" :question/math-academy-id "e-13103" :question/problem "Paul knows that the distance between cell tower $A$ and cell tower $B$ is $35\\,\\textrm{miles}$, and the distance between cell tower $B$ and cell tower $C$ is $25\\,\\textrm{miles}$, but he does not know their exact locations. Assuming that the three locations form a triangle, what is the range of possible values for the distance between cellphone towers $A$ and $C$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we let:\n\n- $A$ be the position of the tower $A$\n- $B$ be the position of the tower $B$\n- $C$ be the position of the tower $C$\n\nLet's draw $\\triangle ABC$.\n\n![](images/a7/a74e768b739cf6ea497af934a44f0a5616da191b7f1a1c0979673a7ca891b0d6.png)\n\nWe know that\n\n$$\nAB = 35\\,\\textrm{mi}, \\qquad BC = 25\\,\\textrm{mi}\n$$\n\nAccording to the triangle inequality theorem,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}AB - BC & < AC < AB + BC \\\\ 35 - 25 & < AC < 35 + 25 \\\\ 10 & < AC < 60\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nTherefore, the distance from the tower $A$ to the tower $C$ must be *between $10 \\,\\textrm{mi}$ and $60 \\,\\textrm{mi}$*."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082924 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083398 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3736cf03-5a3a-5a22-8338-63cdabb1c046" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Upper Bound for a Length"}] :question/id #uuid "1512acb2-ac78-557a-b6a7-5b37addbeb24" :question/math-academy-id "e-13104" :question/problem "![](images/63/6301ab046f34d3261395d10ba73d31f62262c2362d0b088b38f67354568af97e.png)\n\nUsing the triangle inequality theorem only, what is the best upper bound that can be obtained for the length of $\\overline{AC}$?\n(*Note: Figure not drawn to scale*)" :question/worked-solution "The triangle inequality theorem states that the length of any one side must be smaller than the sum of the other two sides.\n\n- Applying the triangle inequality theorem to $\\triangle{ADC}$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}AC & < AD + DC \\\\ AC & < 26cm + 13cm \\\\ AC & < 39cm\\end{bmatrix}$.\n- Applying the triangle inequality theorem to $\\triangle{ABC}$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}AC & < AB + BC \\\\ AC & < 12cm + 10cm \\\\ AC & < 22cm\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nSince both $AC < 39\\,\\textrm{cm}$ and $AC < 22\\,\\textrm{cm}$ must be satisfied, we conclude that\n\n$$\nAC < 22\\,\\textrm{cm}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083834 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083395 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "768204cd-ee1f-5271-9ec9-71d672b4eb9e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Impossible Sides of a Triangle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e637d759-b888-597b-8190-667bccd6624a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11, 8, 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081936} {:answer/id #uuid "f5c8f11c-6162-5297-82fc-39ec782c2317" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17, 15, 8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081937} {:answer/id #uuid "029c6bc9-f4ff-5f9a-9f14-2473ce95a6e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9, 6, 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081938} {:answer/id #uuid "203b1025-ca87-5048-b051-f6afe6ca44b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7, 6, 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081939} {:answer/id #uuid "5339d743-4f83-57b2-bd4f-76c3b1641fe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17, 9, 8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081940}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d5df77ed-7aca-5bb7-9391-a75fd78a101f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081935}] :question/id #uuid "19773a51-267a-5cef-93ba-8a45b8fbcd07" :question/math-academy-id "q-6086" :question/problem "Which three numbers **CANNOT** be the measures of the sides of a triangle?"}]}
